arge of like the imaginary  play section of the day and the school I  worked at had this like stock score  soundtrack so it was like a bunch of  movie soundtracks so every day I would  put on this soundtrack and I'd go to  sleep gonna sleep yeah something like  right in the middle of the carpet I was  like go to bed go to bed  they're like the music would start it  was like and I would just like a league  that was like oh we're in a zoo like  what are you seeing there like I see a  dinosaur like I see a penguin one kids  like I see you later okay it's just like  seeing how young kids can imagine  together it's like so cute you know it's  a veil we're like on board yes there is  a dinosaur in that corner like yes we're  being attacked by bumblebees but they're  nice and like yes there's a race car  running through it you know whatever  they come up with it's just amazing to  see everyone work together and you're  like huh what if we all did that all the  time I think what is the life lesson  that I've taken away from working with  students is that you can just be  yourself and that that's an okay thing  preschoolers they don't have any  preconceived notions of trying to be  something else unless they're playing  pretend or wanting to impress somebody  or needing something so that they can  have status they just are who they are  kids have so much more depth to them  than I think people give them credit for  at the surface the world is made for  adults we don't realize that and it's  all it's easy for a kid to feel very out  of place or like they can't do anything  yet but a lot of these kids they're  they're people they're real they have  real feelings and real ambitions the big  takeaway from working with students and  being a teacher is that kids at their  heart and core are just themselves and  they're really not afraid to be  themselves and to stay like exactly how  they're feeling about everything and I  think as we grow older and we put up  more and more shells